<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint Thomas Christians</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nasrani</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Saint Thomas Christians are the ancient Christian community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>, who trace their origin to the apostle Thomas and long used the East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> liturgy, which is why they are often called Syrian Christians. Their churches held privileges from local rulers, recorded in the Tharisapalli <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plates of 849 for the church at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kollam">Kollam</a>, and they were prominent in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> trade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-thomas-christians.jpg" alt="Saint Thomas Christians" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jogytmathew · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nasrani_cross.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Saint Thomas Christians are an ethno-religious community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>, on the Malabar coast, who speak Malayalam and use <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> in worship. According to tradition, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-the-apostle">Thomas the Apostle</a> came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muziris">Muziris</a>, in present-day Pattanam near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodungallur">Kodungallur</a>, in 52 CE, though historians dispute the visit. The earliest known source linking the apostle with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-thomas">Acts of Thomas</a>, probably written in the early 3rd century, perhaps in Edessa. Roman writers of the 3rd and 4th centuries, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose of Milan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a> and Ephrem the Syrian, also mention his journey. Cochin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> lived in Kerala in the 1st century, so an Aramaic-speaking Jew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> could have made the trip.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>As East Syriac Christians immigrated, the community's ties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a>, centred at Seleucia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, strengthened. From the early 4th century the Patriarch supplied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> with clergy and holy texts, and about 650 Patriarch Ishoyahb III confirmed his jurisdiction. In the 8th century Patriarch Timothy I organised the Ecclesiastical Province of India under a metropolitan sent from Persia, with a native Archdeacon who held authority over the clergy and considerable secular power.</p><p>The community first met the Portuguese in 1498, during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a>'s expedition. Thriving in the spice trade and protected by its own militia, it quickly allied with the newcomers. The Portuguese set up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> hierarchy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> and worked to bring the Saint Thomas Christians under its Archbishop. The Synod of Diamper in 1599 imposed the Roman Rite. The Coonan Cross Oath protest of 1653 led to a permanent schism between the Puthankoor, or New Allegiance, who took the West Syriac Rite in communion with the Syriac Orthodox Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, and the Old Allegiance. In 1815 the British Resident John Munro founded a seminary at Kottayam and invited Anglican missionaries to teach there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Today the community is divided among Eastern Catholic, Oriental Orthodox, Protestant and independent churches, each with its own liturgy. It is also divided into the Vadakkumbhagar and the Tekkumbhagar, also called Knanaya, who trace their descent from the merchant Knai Thoma. Their saying runs that they are Indian in culture, Christian in faith and Syrian in liturgy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-building">Church buildings</a> often followed the model of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples, with a large granite cross in the yard, while the interior follows East Syriac design. The Quilon Syrian copper plates record the building of the church of Tharisapally. Holy Thursday is called Pesaha, from the word for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a>, and the community shares Pesaha Appam after the service.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saint Thomas Christians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Tharisapalli plates of 849, issued under Sthanu Ravi, granted privileges to a <b>Syrian Christian</b> church in Kollam, and the Jewish copper plates of Bhaskara Ravi gave rights to a Jewish merchant.</blockquote><a href="/state/chera-perumals-of-makotai">Chera Perumals of Makotai</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tharisapalli copper plates of 849, in which the Venad chief Ayyanadikal Thiruvadikal granted land and privileges to the <b>Syrian Christian</b> church at Kollam, are among the earliest records of Kerala.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-venad">Kingdom of Venad</a></li></ul></section>
